    "attributes": {
        "input.value": "{\"messages\": [[{\"lc\": 1, \"type\": \"constructor\", \"id\": [\"langchain\", \"schema\", \"messages\", \"SystemMessage\"], \"kwargs\": {\"content\": \"You are a research assistant. Given the research summary and evaluation scores\\nbelow, write a clear, concise final answer for the user. Cite sources inline\\nusing [1], [2], \u2026 notation matching the sources list.\", \"type\": \"system\"}}, {\"lc\": 1, \"type\": \"constructor\", \"id\": [\"langchain\", \"schema\", \"messages\", \"HumanMessage\"], \"kwargs\": {\"content\": \"User query: What is OpenTelemetry and what problem does it solve for distributed systems?\\n\\nResearch summary: OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework designed to provide standardized methods for collecting, processing, and exporting telemetry data such as metrics, logs, and traces from distributed systems. It addresses the challenge of monitoring complex, distributed architectures by offering a unified set of APIs, libraries, and agents that facilitate the collection of telemetry data across various services and platforms. This standardization helps developers and operators gain insights into system performance and behavior, enabling them to diagnose issues, optimize performance, and ensure reliability in distributed environments. OpenTelemetry supports a wide range of programming languages and integrates with numerous back-end systems, making it a versatile tool for observability in modern cloud-native applications.\\n\\nSources:\\n[1] https://www.splunk.com/en_us/blog/learn/opentelemetry.html \u2014 OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework that provides a standardized way to collect, process, and export telemetry data such as metrics, logs, and traces.\\n[2] https://last9.io/blog/opentelemetry-tracing \u2014 OpenTelemetry offers a developer-first control plane to manage logs, metrics, and traces, supporting OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, and over 100 integrations.\\n[3] https://www.dynatrace.com/news/blog/what-is-opentelemetry \u2014 OpenTelemetry provides a unified set of APIs, libraries, and agents to collect telemetry data across various services and platforms, helping to monitor complex, distributed architectures.\\n\\nFaithfulness score: 1.00\", \"type\": \"human\"}}]]}",
        "input.mime_type": "application/json",
        "output.value": "{\"generations\": [[{\"text\": \"OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework that provides standardized methods for collecting, processing, and exporting telemetry data such as metrics, logs, and traces from distributed systems. It solves the problem of monitoring complex, distributed architectures by offering a unified set of APIs, libraries, and agents that facilitate the collection of telemetry data across various services and platforms. This standardization allows developers and operators to gain insights into system performance and behavior, enabling them to diagnose issues, optimize performance, and ensure reliability in distributed environments. OpenTelemetry supports a wide range of programming languages and integrates with numerous back-end systems, making it a versatile tool for observability in modern cloud-native applications [1][2][3].\", \"generation_info\": {\"finish_reason\": \"stop\", \"logprobs\": null}, \"type\": \"ChatGeneration\", \"message\": {\"lc\": 1, \"type\": \"constructor\", \"id\": [\"langchain\", \"schema\", \"messages\", \"AIMessage\"], \"kwargs\": {\"content\": \"OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework that provides standardized methods for collecting, processing, and exporting telemetry data such as metrics, logs, and traces from distributed systems. It solves the problem of monitoring complex, distributed architectures by offering a unified set of APIs, libraries, and agents that facilitate the collection of telemetry data across various services and platforms. This standardization allows developers and operators to gain insights into system performance and behavior, enabling them to diagnose issues, optimize performance, and ensure reliability in distributed environments. OpenTelemetry supports a wide range of programming languages and integrates with numerous back-end systems, making it a versatile tool for observability in modern cloud-native applications [1][2][3].\", \"additional_kwargs\": {\"refusal\": null}, \"response_metadata\": {\"token_usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": 138, \"prompt_tokens\": 369, \"total_tokens\": 507, \"completion_tokens_details\": {\"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0, \"audio_tokens\": 0, \"reasoning_tokens\": 0, \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0}, \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\"audio_tokens\": 0, \"cached_tokens\": 0}}, \"model_provider\": \"openai\", \"model_name\": \"gpt-4o-2024-08-06\", \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_a7e0560a58\", \"id\": \"chatcmpl-DvsLyoIhgfO1XbL29QAhsq6PrVua1\", \"service_tier\": \"default\", \"finish_reason\": \"stop\", \"logprobs\": null}, \"type\": \"ai\", \"id\": \"lc_run--019f105d-ad7c-73a3-a591-fdb5355bae16-0\", \"usage_metadata\": {\"input_tokens\": 369, \"output_tokens\": 138, \"total_tokens\": 507, \"input_token_details\": {\"audio\": 0, \"cache_read\": 0}, \"output_token_details\": {\"audio\": 0, \"reasoning\": 0}}, \"tool_calls\": [], \"invalid_tool_calls\": []}}}]], \"llm_output\": {\"token_usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": 138, \"prompt_tokens\": 369, \"total_tokens\": 507, \"completion_tokens_details\": {\"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0, \"audio_tokens\": 0, \"reasoning_tokens\": 0, \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0}, \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\"audio_tokens\": 0, \"cached_tokens\": 0}}, \"model_provider\": \"openai\", \"model_name\": \"gpt-4o-2024-08-06\", \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_a7e0560a58\", \"id\": \"chatcmpl-DvsLyoIhgfO1XbL29QAhsq6PrVua1\", \"service_tier\": \"default\"}, \"run\": null, \"type\": \"LLMResult\"}",
        "output.mime_type": "application/json",
        "llm.input_messages.0.message.role": "system",
        "llm.input_messages.0.message.content": "You are a research assistant. Given the research summary and evaluation scores\nbelow, write a clear, concise final answer for the user. Cite sources inline\nusing [1], [2], \u2026 notation matching the sources list.",
        "llm.input_messages.1.message.role": "user",
        "llm.input_messages.1.message.content": "User query: What is OpenTelemetry and what problem does it solve for distributed systems?\n\nResearch summary: OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework designed to provide standardized methods for collecting, processing, and exporting telemetry data such as metrics, logs, and traces from distributed systems. It addresses the challenge of monitoring complex, distributed architectures by offering a unified set of APIs, libraries, and agents that facilitate the collection of telemetry data across various services and platforms. This standardization helps developers and operators gain insights into system performance and behavior, enabling them to diagnose issues, optimize performance, and ensure reliability in distributed environments. OpenTelemetry supports a wide range of programming languages and integrates with numerous back-end systems, making it a versatile tool for observability in modern cloud-native applications.\n\nSources:\n[1] https://www.splunk.com/en_us/blog/learn/opentelemetry.html \u2014 OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework that provides a standardized way to collect, process, and export telemetry data such as metrics, logs, and traces.\n[2] https://last9.io/blog/opentelemetry-tracing \u2014 OpenTelemetry offers a developer-first control plane to manage logs, metrics, and traces, supporting OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, and over 100 integrations.\n[3] https://www.dynatrace.com/news/blog/what-is-opentelemetry \u2014 OpenTelemetry provides a unified set of APIs, libraries, and agents to collect telemetry data across various services and platforms, helping to monitor complex, distributed architectures.\n\nFaithfulness score: 1.00",
        "llm.output_messages.0.message.role": "assistant",
        "llm.output_messages.0.message.content": "OpenTelemetry is an open-source observability framework that provides standardized methods for collecting, processing, and exporting telemetry data such as metrics, logs, and traces from distributed systems. It solves the problem of monitoring complex, distributed architectures by offering a unified set of APIs, libraries, and agents that facilitate the collection of telemetry data across various services and platforms. This standardization allows developers and operators to gain insights into system performance and behavior, enabling them to diagnose issues, optimize performance, and ensure reliability in distributed environments. OpenTelemetry supports a wide range of programming languages and integrates with numerous back-end systems, making it a versatile tool for observability in modern cloud-native applications [1][2][3].",
        "llm.invocation_parameters": "{\"model\": \"gpt-4o\", \"model_name\": \"gpt-4o\", \"stream\": false, \"temperature\": 0.0, \"_type\": \"openai-chat\", \"stop\": null}",
