    "attributes": {
        "input.value": "{\"messages\": [[{\"lc\": 1, \"type\": \"constructor\", \"id\": [\"langchain\", \"schema\", \"messages\", \"SystemMessage\"], \"kwargs\": {\"content\": \"You are a research assistant. Given the research summary and evaluation scores\\nbelow, write a clear, concise final answer for the user. Cite sources inline\\nusing [1], [2], \u2026 notation matching the sources list.\", \"type\": \"system\"}}, {\"lc\": 1, \"type\": \"constructor\", \"id\": [\"langchain\", \"schema\", \"messages\", \"HumanMessage\"], \"kwargs\": {\"content\": \"User query: What are the main observability requirements for a multi-agent LLM system? Which open-source tools best address those requirements in 2025, and what are their key limitations?\\n\\nResearch summary: The main observability requirements for a multi-agent LLM (Large Language Model) system include monitoring, debugging, evaluation, and performance tracking. These systems need to handle complex interactions between multiple agents, requiring tools that can provide insights into model behavior, data flow, and system performance. In 2025, several open-source tools are noted for addressing these requirements effectively. According to Braintrust, some of the leading tools include MLflow, Prometheus, and Grafana. MLflow is praised for its comprehensive experiment tracking and model evaluation capabilities, but it may lack real-time monitoring features. Prometheus is strong in real-time monitoring but can be complex to set up and manage. Grafana excels in visualization but relies on data sources like Prometheus for metrics collection. Each tool has its limitations, such as scalability issues or integration challenges, which users must consider when implementing them in a multi-agent LLM system.\\n\\nSources:\\n[1] https://mlflow.org/top-5-agent-observability-tools \u2014 MLflow is highlighted for its features in experiment tracking and model evaluation, which are crucial for observability in LLM systems.\\n[2] https://www.braintrust.dev/articles/top-10-llm-observability-tools-2025 \u2014 Prometheus and Grafana are noted for their strengths in real-time monitoring and visualization, respectively, but each has limitations such as complexity and dependency on other tools.\\n[3] https://www.onpage.com/top-12-ai-and-llm-observability-tools-in-2026-compared-open-source-and-paid \u2014 The article compares various tools, emphasizing the need for comprehensive monitoring and debugging capabilities in multi-agent LLM systems.\\n\\nFaithfulness score: 1.00\", \"type\": \"human\"}}]]}",
        "input.mime_type": "application/json",
        "output.value": "{\"generations\": [[{\"text\": \"The main observability requirements for a multi-agent LLM system include monitoring, debugging, evaluation, and performance tracking. These systems need tools that can provide insights into model behavior, data flow, and system performance due to the complexity of interactions between multiple agents.\\n\\nIn 2025, some of the best open-source tools addressing these requirements are MLflow, Prometheus, and Grafana. MLflow is noted for its comprehensive experiment tracking and model evaluation capabilities, which are crucial for observability in LLM systems, though it may lack real-time monitoring features [1]. Prometheus excels in real-time monitoring but can be complex to set up and manage [2]. Grafana is strong in visualization but depends on data sources like Prometheus for metrics collection [2]. Each tool has limitations, such as scalability issues or integration challenges, which should be considered when implementing them in a multi-agent LLM system [3].\", \"generation_info\": {\"finish_reason\": \"stop\", \"logprobs\": null}, \"type\": \"ChatGeneration\", \"message\": {\"lc\": 1, \"type\": \"constructor\", \"id\": [\"langchain\", \"schema\", \"messages\", \"AIMessage\"], \"kwargs\": {\"content\": \"The main observability requirements for a multi-agent LLM system include monitoring, debugging, evaluation, and performance tracking. These systems need tools that can provide insights into model behavior, data flow, and system performance due to the complexity of interactions between multiple agents.\\n\\nIn 2025, some of the best open-source tools addressing these requirements are MLflow, Prometheus, and Grafana. MLflow is noted for its comprehensive experiment tracking and model evaluation capabilities, which are crucial for observability in LLM systems, though it may lack real-time monitoring features [1]. Prometheus excels in real-time monitoring but can be complex to set up and manage [2]. Grafana is strong in visualization but depends on data sources like Prometheus for metrics collection [2]. Each tool has limitations, such as scalability issues or integration challenges, which should be considered when implementing them in a multi-agent LLM system [3].\", \"additional_kwargs\": {\"refusal\": null}, \"response_metadata\": {\"token_usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": 182, \"prompt_tokens\": 441, \"total_tokens\": 623, \"completion_tokens_details\": {\"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0, \"audio_tokens\": 0, \"reasoning_tokens\": 0, \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0}, \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\"audio_tokens\": 0, \"cached_tokens\": 0}}, \"model_provider\": \"openai\", \"model_name\": \"gpt-4o-2024-08-06\", \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_683410201e\", \"id\": \"chatcmpl-DvsMdlccWnJwpyFL4Sbgp7phcyoPB\", \"service_tier\": \"default\", \"finish_reason\": \"stop\", \"logprobs\": null}, \"type\": \"ai\", \"id\": \"lc_run--019f105e-4d8c-7ad1-afcc-d988f82abfd5-0\", \"usage_metadata\": {\"input_tokens\": 441, \"output_tokens\": 182, \"total_tokens\": 623, \"input_token_details\": {\"audio\": 0, \"cache_read\": 0}, \"output_token_details\": {\"audio\": 0, \"reasoning\": 0}}, \"tool_calls\": [], \"invalid_tool_calls\": []}}}]], \"llm_output\": {\"token_usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": 182, \"prompt_tokens\": 441, \"total_tokens\": 623, \"completion_tokens_details\": {\"accepted_prediction_tokens\": 0, \"audio_tokens\": 0, \"reasoning_tokens\": 0, \"rejected_prediction_tokens\": 0}, \"prompt_tokens_details\": {\"audio_tokens\": 0, \"cached_tokens\": 0}}, \"model_provider\": \"openai\", \"model_name\": \"gpt-4o-2024-08-06\", \"system_fingerprint\": \"fp_683410201e\", \"id\": \"chatcmpl-DvsMdlccWnJwpyFL4Sbgp7phcyoPB\", \"service_tier\": \"default\"}, \"run\": null, \"type\": \"LLMResult\"}",
        "output.mime_type": "application/json",
        "llm.input_messages.0.message.role": "system",
        "llm.input_messages.0.message.content": "You are a research assistant. Given the research summary and evaluation scores\nbelow, write a clear, concise final answer for the user. Cite sources inline\nusing [1], [2], \u2026 notation matching the sources list.",
        "llm.input_messages.1.message.role": "user",
        "llm.input_messages.1.message.content": "User query: What are the main observability requirements for a multi-agent LLM system? Which open-source tools best address those requirements in 2025, and what are their key limitations?\n\nResearch summary: The main observability requirements for a multi-agent LLM (Large Language Model) system include monitoring, debugging, evaluation, and performance tracking. These systems need to handle complex interactions between multiple agents, requiring tools that can provide insights into model behavior, data flow, and system performance. In 2025, several open-source tools are noted for addressing these requirements effectively. According to Braintrust, some of the leading tools include MLflow, Prometheus, and Grafana. MLflow is praised for its comprehensive experiment tracking and model evaluation capabilities, but it may lack real-time monitoring features. Prometheus is strong in real-time monitoring but can be complex to set up and manage. Grafana excels in visualization but relies on data sources like Prometheus for metrics collection. Each tool has its limitations, such as scalability issues or integration challenges, which users must consider when implementing them in a multi-agent LLM system.\n\nSources:\n[1] https://mlflow.org/top-5-agent-observability-tools \u2014 MLflow is highlighted for its features in experiment tracking and model evaluation, which are crucial for observability in LLM systems.\n[2] https://www.braintrust.dev/articles/top-10-llm-observability-tools-2025 \u2014 Prometheus and Grafana are noted for their strengths in real-time monitoring and visualization, respectively, but each has limitations such as complexity and dependency on other tools.\n[3] https://www.onpage.com/top-12-ai-and-llm-observability-tools-in-2026-compared-open-source-and-paid \u2014 The article compares various tools, emphasizing the need for comprehensive monitoring and debugging capabilities in multi-agent LLM systems.\n\nFaithfulness score: 1.00",
        "llm.output_messages.0.message.role": "assistant",
        "llm.output_messages.0.message.content": "The main observability requirements for a multi-agent LLM system include monitoring, debugging, evaluation, and performance tracking. These systems need tools that can provide insights into model behavior, data flow, and system performance due to the complexity of interactions between multiple agents.\n\nIn 2025, some of the best open-source tools addressing these requirements are MLflow, Prometheus, and Grafana. MLflow is noted for its comprehensive experiment tracking and model evaluation capabilities, which are crucial for observability in LLM systems, though it may lack real-time monitoring features [1]. Prometheus excels in real-time monitoring but can be complex to set up and manage [2]. Grafana is strong in visualization but depends on data sources like Prometheus for metrics collection [2]. Each tool has limitations, such as scalability issues or integration challenges, which should be considered when implementing them in a multi-agent LLM system [3].",
        "llm.invocation_parameters": "{\"model\": \"gpt-4o\", \"model_name\": \"gpt-4o\", \"stream\": false, \"temperature\": 0.0, \"_type\": \"openai-chat\", \"stop\": null}",
